# Problem and quantifiers

For a unit-separated finite set in the plane define

\[
D(n)=\min\{\operatorname{diam}(A):A\subset\mathbb R^2,\ |A|=n,\ |x-y|\ge1\}.
\]

Erdos Problem #99 asks whether there is an n_0 such that for every n >= n_0,
**every** global minimizer contains a unit equilateral triangle.

A proof that some minimizer contains a triangle is insufficient.
A local triangle-free packing is insufficient for a disproof.
A disproof requires infinitely many triangle-free **global minimizers**.

Never call an asymptotic, local, KKT, or numerically observed configuration a global
counterexample without an exact global certificate.

---

# Authoritative Target A endpoint

For the plateau-terminalized scaffold let

\[
\zeta=\dim\ker S=|C^\partial|-\operatorname{rank}S,
\qquad
s=\Delta_\Lambda-d_{\rm int}.
\]

A surviving hypothetical counterexample has

\[
\boxed{
g=\Delta_\Lambda-d_{\rm sc}=s-\zeta=\rho^\dagger-\Phi(v)\ge1.
}
\]

Target A closes exactly if you prove

\[
\boxed{\zeta\ge s,}
\]

or equivalently

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{rank}S
\le d_{\rm int}+|C^\partial|-\Delta_\Lambda.
}
\]

The Schur response is an exact planar strict gammoid.  Its rank is the maximum number
of vertex-disjoint directed alternating paths from the boundary source set to exterior
lattice sinks, and also the minimum directed vertex separator.  Planarity alone is
not enough.

