# Exact fixed-container capacity endpoint

For any unit-separated P subset H with |P|=q and M=alpha_1(H), and any
capacity-maximizing X_max subset H with |X_max|=M, the strict conflict graph

\[
p\sim x\iff |p-x|<1
\]

has a matching saturating P:

\[
\boxed{\nu(P,X_{\max})=|P|.}
\]

Moreover

\[
\boxed{
\max_Q\bigl(|Q|-|N_P(Q)|\bigr)=M-|P|.
}
\]

Thus capacity bookkeeping alone can NEVER produce deficiency greater than its own
available capacity slack.  A proof of Target A must use the global relation to the
smaller optimum, a triangle-free/global-optimality obstruction, or an exact
transition mechanism.

For the normalized B, every q-subset of X_max has diameter D.  The diameter graph of
X_max has vertex-cover number at least tau+1, and X_max has at least tau+1 points on
\(\partial H\).

A capacity endpoint is completely saturated relative to H in the finite cardinality
sense: no r-1 of its points can be replaced by r points in H for any r>=1.

---

# Major v1.7 theorem: source-capacity plateau exchange flag

Fix B and one capacity endpoint X_max.  Present the strict conflict graph as a
transversal matroid on X_max with right shore B.  Choose a maximum basis \mathcal B of
size q.  The nonbasis set has size tau.

For each nonbasis element e let C_e be its fundamental circuit.  For any subset A_0
of the nonbasis elements put

\[
U_{A_0}=\bigcup_{e\in A_0}C_e,
\qquad
W_{A_0}=N_B(U_{A_0}).
\]

The fixed-basis theorem gives

\[
\boxed{|U_{A_0}|-|W_{A_0}|=|A_0|.}
\]

Then

\[
P_{A_0}=(B\setminus W_{A_0})\cup U_{A_0}
\]

is a global optimum with

\[
|P_{A_0}|=q+|A_0|,
\qquad
\operatorname{diam}P_{A_0}=D.
\]

Ordering the tau nonbasis elements yields an exact adjacent-cardinality flag

\[
B=P_0\rightsquigarrow P_1\rightsquigarrow\cdots\rightsquigarrow P_\tau,
\]

where every step satisfies

\[
P_i=(P_{i-1}\setminus\Delta B_i)\cup\Delta X_i,
\qquad
|\Delta X_i|=|\Delta B_i|+1.
\]

This is an explicit chain of global optima at every cardinality between q and
alpha_1(H).

If every P_i is triangle-free, the chain reaches a triangle-free same-hull capacity
endpoint with zero capacity slack.

Otherwise let j be the first triangle-producing step.  Every new triangle contains a
point of Delta X_j.  The proof problem can therefore be localized to ONE exact
Hall-tight adjacent plateau transition.

Use this theorem aggressively.  Do not return to statements like "optima at different
cardinalities are completely unrelated" without acknowledging this source-capacity
exception.

---

# Fundamental-circuit geometry

For any fundamental circuit C with right neighborhood W,

\[
|C|=|W|+1,
\]

its induced strict conflict graph is connected, and every right vertex has degree at
least two.

Strict conflict graphs between two internally unit-separated shores are planar.
Cross-shore coincident pairs, if any, are isolated K_2 components; a nontrivial
fundamental circuit has no such pair.  Define

\[
\kappa=e-2|W|.
\]

Then

\[
\boxed{
\kappa
=\sum_{w\in W}(\deg w-2)
=\#\{\text{bounded faces of the circuit graph}\}.
}
\]

If kappa=0, the circuit graph is a tree, every right vertex has degree two, and the
entire net-+1 exchange sequentializes into one-for-one feasible swaps followed by one
final append.

Every intermediate same-cardinality set remains a global optimum.  It need not remain
triangle-free.

This gives two sharply different first-transition branches:

1. tree circuit: analyze a sequential swap chain and the first exact triangle event;
2. cyclic circuit: charge the obstruction to the exact planar face budget kappa.

A strong theorem eliminating both branches would be major progress.

