# Finite-chord stress identity

Let p_i be the source coordinates, y_i=mu(p_i), and v_i=y_i-p_i.  For any normalized
proper source KKT stress (alpha,beta),

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{ij\in C}\alpha_{ij}|v_i-v_j|^2
\ge
\sum_{ij\in H_D}\beta_{ij}|v_i-v_j|^2.
}
\]

More exactly,

\[
\begin{aligned}
&\sum_C\alpha_{ij}(|y_i-y_j|^2-1)
+\sum_{H_D}\beta_{ij}(D^2-|y_i-y_j|^2)\\
&=\sum_C\alpha_{ij}|v_i-v_j|^2
-\sum_{H_D}\beta_{ij}|v_i-v_j|^2\ge0.
\end{aligned}
\]

Equality means every positively stressed source contact and diameter edge remains
active in the capacity shadow.

This is finite-amplitude, not merely local KKT.  However, strict rattlers themselves
have no active stress, so do not assert a positive lower bound from rattler count
without a new theorem.

---

# Affine forest triangle barriers

The Section 62 factor theorem remains authoritative: every codimension-one triangle
wall in the affine forest flat has an identically unit side in the fixed part.

v1.7 sharpens this.  If the third vertex a is moving, its displacement map has rank

\[
\dim\operatorname{span}\{u_e:e\text{ lies on the root path to }a\}.
\]

Therefore:

\[
\boxed{
\text{a codimension-one triangle wall involving a moving vertex is possible only if
its entire retained root path is collinear.}
}
\]

If the path contains two nonparallel unit-edge directions, every triangle locus
involving that vertex has codimension at least two.

Thus only straight-chain prefixes can separate the affine forest parameter space by
codimension-one triangle walls.  A promising source-side theorem is to show that a
global minimizer cannot hide a super-boundary number of forest degrees of freedom
behind such straight-chain barriers.

Do not assume long straight rooted chains are impossible; they remain valid local
regressions.

