# Highest-priority new attacks

## A. Finite monomer gap-concentration theorem

The scaffold's only Hall-neutral bulk phases are monomer and dimer phases.  A coherent
monomer phase is literally a bounded-displacement deformation of the triangular
lattice.

Prove a finite analogue of the Kuperberg gap mechanism:

> A sufficiently large coherent monomer domain in the globally derived scaffold, with
> positive distributed nearest-neighbor clearance and only boundary-order total
> clearance, admits a finite rearrangement inside the relevant hull/collar that frees
> one additional unit-separated site without increasing diameter.

This would directly kill the principal strained-monomer regression.  Boundary
conditions and simultaneous feasibility are the hard part.  Do not replace them by
an infinite-plane statement.

## B. First triangle-producing exchange transition

Take the first j in the exact source-capacity flag for which P_j contains a unit
triangle.

Prove either:

1. a different circuit ordering, basis exchange, or small perturbation gives a
   triangle-free global optimum at the same new cardinality; or
2. the first-triangle support forces at least s independent failures of the target
   alternating linkage, i.e. dim ker S >= s.

Start with the tree-circuit case, where the transition sequentializes into simple
swaps.  Then treat cyclic circuits using kappa, the exact bounded-face budget.

## C. Capacity-shadow to Schur transport

Use the pointwise <1 matching A -> Y and the finite-chord stress identity.  Delete the
strict-rattler population and compare the resulting matched backbone to a
right-plateau smaller optimum.  Seek an exact map into ker S, not an O(D) estimate.

## D. Straight-chain barrier elimination

Combine branchwise core-face confinement, source cage estimates, and the new
collinear-path triangle-wall theorem.  Prove that only O(D) independent forest
coordinates can lie behind codimension-one straight-chain barriers, contradicting a
super-boundary flexible branch.

## E. Direct Schur separator theorem

You may still attack the canonical endpoint directly.  In the hard case

\[
s=\Delta_\Lambda-d_{\rm int}\ge1,
\]

prove a directed alternating separator of size at most

\[
|C^\partial|-s.
\]

But do not use pure planarity, width-one-annulus assumptions, or accidental determinant
cancellation.  The generic Schur minors are rational functions whose numerators are
structural matching/linkage determinants.

